From 540881b8783142c911ccb6037d9410678acf39b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philip Dye <phdye@acm.org>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:45:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Makerules: give the stdio_lim dependency file a rule of
 its own

Under GNU make 4.4.1 the build never leaves stdio-common: make re-executes
itself in that subdirectory without end. bits/stdio_lim.h and
bits/stdio_lim.d shared one pattern rule whose recipe is a no-op, both
depending on the stamp that is touched after they are written. Make 4.3
and later treat the targets of a multi-target pattern rule as a group, so
remaking the header, which happens on every pass since the stamp is
newer, marks the .d file remade too. The .d file is an included makefile,
so make reads everything again, and the next pass does the same. el8 built
this tree with make 4.2, which did not group them.

Two rules instead of one. The .d file's own rule leaves its timestamp the
only thing make consults, and it has not changed. Reproduced and confirmed
with make -d on the stdio-common subdirectory: twenty-five re-executions in
twenty seconds before, one after.
---
 Makerules | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Makerules b/Makerules
index 05a549eb..4774c3e3 100644
--- a/Makerules
+++ b/Makerules
@@ -1541,7 +1541,14 @@ L_cuserid ?= 1
 
 stdio_lim = $(common-objpfx)bits/stdio_lim.h
 
-$(stdio_lim:lim.h=%.h) $(stdio_lim:lim.h=%.d): $(stdio_lim:lim.h=%.st); @:
+# Two rules rather than one pattern rule naming both.  The .d file is an
+# included makefile, and GNU make 4.3 and later treat the targets of a
+# multi-target pattern rule as a group: remaking the header's no-op recipe
+# marks the .d file remade as well, make re-executes to read it again, and
+# the next pass does the same, forever.  A rule of its own leaves the .d
+# file's timestamp the only thing make consults, and it has not changed.
+$(stdio_lim): $(stdio_lim:h=st); @:
+$(stdio_lim:h=d): $(stdio_lim:h=st); @:
 $(stdio_lim:h=st): $(..)stdio-common/stdio_lim.h.in $(..)Rules \
 		   $(common-objpfx)config.make
 	$(make-target-directory)
